   "text": "I read the structure as a macrocyclic peptide-like molecule and built the SMILES from its parts.\n\n**Fragments**\n- **Ornithine residue:** NH2\u2013(CH2)3\u2013CH, with its \u03b1-NH linked through a CH2 to the 3-position of a pyridine. The drawing shows three chain CH2 groups, so this is ornithine.\n- **Lysine residue:** NH2\u2013(CH2)4\u2013CH, whose \u03b1-NH is acylated by the ornithine C=O. There are four chain CH2 groups.\n- **N-methyl tryptophan:** the lysine C=O joins the N-methyl nitrogen. The side chain is CH2 to indol-3-yl.\n- **Pyridine to benzene link:** the pyridine carries S at C2 and CH2NH at C3. The sulfur joins a benzene ring.\n- **Benzene substituents:** the ring carries S, CH2NH (which closes the macrocycle onto the Trp C=O), a 4-pyridon-1-yl-acetic acid, and Cl.\n- **Pyridone:** N\u2013CH2COOH, C2=O, and the aryl group at C4.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\nAll three \u03b1-carbons are drawn with wedges and each works out as S, matching the L-amino-acid configuration. I checked each centre by CIP priorities (N > C=O > CH2, with H pointing back). That gives `[C@H]` for ornithine and `[C@@H]` for both lysine and tryptophan in the SMILES order I wrote.\n\nSMILES: NCCC[C@H]1C(=O)N[C@@H](CCCCN)C(=O)N(C)[C@@H](Cc2c[nH]c3ccccc23)C(=O)NCc2c(Sc3ncccc3CN1)c(Cl)ccc2-c1ccn(CC(=O)O)c(=O)c1"
